Actually, so-called \emph{Yaglom reversibility} holds. From \eqref{Jcomm}, it follows that
\begin{equation}
\label{yaglom}
G^{*}=JGJ.
\end{equation}
This identity means that the stationary forward process $(-\infty,\infty)\ni t\mapsto\eta(t)$ and the \emph{flipped backward process}
\begin{equation}
\label{revproc}
(-\infty,\infty)\ni t\mapsto\wt\eta(t):=-\eta(-t)
\end{equation}
obey the same law. This is a special kind of time-reversal symmetry called Yaglom reversibility, see \cite{yaglom_47}, \cite{yaglom_49}, \cite{dobrushin_suhov_fritz_88}.

