We claim that there exists a constant $c>0$ such that
\begin{eqnarray}\label{kurak}
\breve{s}_{g(t)}:=\min_{x\in X}s_{g(t)}(x)< -c.
\end{eqnarray}
Suppose not. Then for any $\epsilon>0$, there exists $t_{\epsilon}>0$ such that $$\breve{s}_{g(t_{\epsilon})}\geq -\epsilon .$$ Note that $g(t)$ for any $t$ is also $G$-invariant by the uniqueness of the Ricci flow. Thus there exists a solution $(A_t, \Phi_t)$ of the Seiberg-Witten equations for $(X,\frak{s})$ with respect to $g(t)$. Thus
\begin{eqnarray*}
(c_1^{+_t}(\frak{s}))^2&\leq& \frac{1}{4\pi^2}\int_X |F_{A_t}^{+_t}|^2 d\mu_{g(t)}\\ &=& \frac{1}{4\pi^2}\int_X |\Phi_t\otimes\Phi_t^*-\frac{|\Phi_t|^2}{2}\textrm{Id}|^2 d\mu_{g(t)}\\ &=& \frac{1}{4\pi^2}\int_X \frac{|\Phi_t|^4}{8} d\mu_{g(t)}.
\end{eqnarray*}
By the well-known Weitzenb\"{o}ck argument, $$|\Phi_t|\leq \max \{-\breve{s}_{g(t)},0 \},$$ and hence $|\Phi_{t_{\epsilon}}|\leq \epsilon.$
Therefore $$(c_1^{+_{t_\epsilon}}(\frak{s}))^2\leq \frac{1}{4\pi^2}\int_X\frac{\epsilon^4}{8} d\mu_{g(t)}=\frac{\epsilon^4}{32\pi^2}\int_Xd\mu_{g(0)},$$ because the normalized Ricci flow preserves the volume. Since $\epsilon>0$ is arbitrary, this yields a contradiction to $\liminf_{t\rightarrow \infty}(c_1^{+_t}(\frak{s}))^2>0.$

