Distribution for difference of two independent variates, $u=x_1-x_2$ , is
	\begin{align}
		P(u)&=\mathcal{N} \int_0^{\Lambda}x_1^{-1+k_1}x_2^{-1+k_2}\delta\left(u-x_1+x_2\right){\rm d} x_1 {\rm d} x_2\nonumber\\
		&\propto
		\begin{cases}\displaystyle
			\int_{0}^{\Lambda-u}\left(u+x_2\right)^{-1+k_1}x_2^{-1+k_2}{\rm d}x_2 & u>0\\\displaystyle
			\int_{-u}^{\Lambda}\left(u+x_2\right)^{-1+k_1}x_2^{-1+k_2}{\rm d}x_2 & u<0
		\end{cases}\nonumber\\
		&\overset{u\to 0^{\pm}}{\propto}
		\begin{cases}
			u^{-1+k_1+k_2}+\mathcal{O}\left(u^0\right) & u>0\\
			(-u)^{-1+k_1+k_2}+\mathcal{O}\left((-u)^{0}\right) & u<0
		\end{cases}\;.
	\end{align}
	Usually we only consider the case $u>0$ and this gives 
	\begin{equation}
		k_u=k_1+k_2\;,
	\end{equation}
	which is the same as summation of two independent variates.

