In 2006, Ben Arous and Voiculescu \cite{BV2006} introduced the free analogue of max-convolution. Given two measures $
u_1, 
u_2 \in \MM(r)$, their \emph{free max-convolution}, denoted by $
u_1 oxlor 
u_2$, is the measures with cumulative distribution function given by
\[
F_{
u_1 oxlor 
u_2}(x) :=\max\{F_{
u_1}(x)+F_{
u_2}(x)-1,0\} \qquad	ext{for all } x\in \mathbb{R}.
\]
Similarly, given $
u \in \MM(r)$ and $t\geq 1$, one can define the \emph{free max-convolution of $
u$ to the power $t$} as the unique measure $
u^{ oxlor t}$ with cumulative distribution function given by
 egin{align}\label{eq:freemaxpower}
F_{
u^{ oxlor t}}(x):=\max\{tF_
u(x)-(t-1),0\}, \qquad t\ge 1.
\end{align}
This notion was introduced by Ueda, who used  Tucci-Haagerup-M"{o}ller's limit to relate it to free additive convolution powers in \cite[Theorem 1.1]{ueda2021max}: given $t\geq 1$ and $\mu\in \MM(r_{\geq 0})$, one has that
 egin{equation}\label{eq:Ueda}
\Phi(\dil{1/t}(\mu^{ oxplus t}) )=\Phi(\mu)^{ oxlor t}.
\end{equation}

